  "strategy": "Approach and rationale\nThe current approach uses random initializations and local optimization (SLSQP) on a fixed 280-point discretization. This is susceptible to getting trapped in local optima. The new approach uses a hierarchical optimization strategy: start from the initial solution and optimize using a faster correlation via FFT, then explore nearby solutions around it, and finally use a genetic algorithm to escape local optima. This reduces the time spent on less promising regions while leveraging the initial solution's proximity to the global optimum. The FFT acceleration and careful exploration are expected to find a solution closer to the 0.380876 bound.\n\nMathematical specification\nRepresentation: A piecewise-constant function discretized into 280 points on [0,2].\nObjective: Minimize C_5 = max_k \u222b h(x)(1-h(x+k)) dx, computed via full cross-correlation with FFT scaling.\nConstraints: 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 for all i, and \u2211h[i] = n_points/2.\nFormulation: The constrained optimization is handled via bounds and an equality constraint, ensuring feasibility by clipping and scaling.\nDomain: n_points = 280 fixed for consistency.\n\nImplementation procedure\n1. Parent validation:\n   - Check if initial_h_values exists; if present, use it as the best candidate.\n2. Objective calculation:\n   - Correlation: use scipy.signal.correlate(method='fft') for faster computation.\n   - C_5 = max(correlation) * dx.\n3. Hierarchical optimization:\n   a. Local refinement:\n      i. Use initial_h_values as starting point.\n      ii. Run trust-constrained optimizer with bounds and sum constraint.\n      iii. Update best candidate if superior.\n   b. Local diversification:\n      i. Start from the optimized parent.\n      ii. Perform small random perturbations to explore nearby solutions.\n      iii. Clip and rescale to maintain constraints.\n   c. Global search:\n      i. Use a genetic algorithm to search the entire space.\n      ii. Crossover: blend parent and offspring solutions.\n      iii. Mutation: small random changes.\n      iv. Repair: ensure sum constraint via clipping and scaling.\n4. Optimization stopping:\n   - Time checks: stop local optimization after max 10 seconds.\n   - Return best candidate after 100 seconds.\n\nSearch choices and useful variations\n1. FFT vs. direct correlation: FFT is faster but still accurate.\n2. Optimization levels: Local refinement (gradient-based) to global search (genetic algorithm).\n3. Initializations: Start from the parent, then its refined version, then random initialization for genetic algorithm.\n4. Local vs. global exploration: Refined local search (100-500 evaluations) followed by an aggressive genetic search (500-1000 evaluations).\n\nComputebudget and robustness\n1. Dominant cost:\n   - Correlation: O(n log n) with FFT vs. O(n^2) with direct.\n   - Initial optimization: 10 seconds (500 evaluations).\n   - Genetic: 10-90 seconds (500-1000 evaluations).\n2. Feasibility checks:\n   - Ensure sum(h) = n_points/2 after each operation.\n   - Skip evaluations if nans/inf detected.\n3. Number of evaluations: Baselined 1000 evaluations at full speed.\n4. C_5 evaluation: Only evaluated if candidate meets feasibility.\n\nValidation and return contract\n1. Feasibility: Check 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 and sum(h) == n_points/2.\n2. Objective computation: Use FFT-correlation with dx scaling.\n3. Return checks: Best candidate must be feasible, score finite.\n4. Objective consistency: Re-evaluated before returning.\n5. final h_values must have exactly n_points elements.",
